      <h2 align="center">Experiment 1 : Buck Converter</h2>
          <div class="container-fluid">
              <h4> Concept </h4>
                  <p>DC-DC Converters are power electronics circuits that convert a dc input voltage to a different dc output voltage level.</p>
                  <p> 
                    <figure class="figure" align="center">
                      <img alt="Fig1" src="_images\fig1.jpg"/>
                      <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 1: A buck converter on a printed circuit board</small></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                  
                  
                    Advantages of dc-dc converters are:
                    <ul>
                      <li>High efficiency.</li>
                      <li>Smaller components and less thermal management.</li>
                      <li>Output voltage can be either higher or lower than the input voltage.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    A buck converter’s output voltage is always lower than the input voltage. Figure 2 shows the schematics of a buck converter.
                    It has a dc input voltage, a transistor working as a switch, an inductor and a capacitor forming a low pass filter to smooth
                    out the output voltage, and a load resistor. The diode provides a path for the inductor current when the switch is opened and
                    is reverse biased when the switch is closed.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <figure class="figure" align=center>
                      <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig2" src="_images\fig2.jpg"/>
                      <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 2. Schematics of a buck converter</small></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                  </p>
                  <p>When the switch is closed, the diode is reverse biased. The equivalent circuit is shown in Figure 3.</p>
                  <p>
                    <figure class="figure" align=center>
                      <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig3" src="_images\fig3.jpg"/>
                      <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 3. Equivalent circuit when the switch is closed</small></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                  </p>
                  <p>When the switch is open, the diode is forward biased. The equivalent circuit is shown in Figure 4.</p>
                  <p>
                    <figure class="figure" align="center">
                      <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig4" src="_images\fig4.jpg"/>
                      <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 4. Equivalent circuit when the switch is open</small></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                  </p>
                  <p>Output voltage of a buck converter is controlled by a pulse width modulated (PWM) signal. By adjusting the duty cycle, we can
                    obtain desired output voltage.</p>
                  <p>
                    <figure class="figure" align=center>
                      <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig5" src="_images\fig5.jpg"/>
                    </figure>
                  </p>
              <h4> Learning Objective </h4>
                <p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>Characteristics of buck converter</li>
                    <li>Capacitor and inductor selection</li>
                    <li>Output voltage and current</li>
                    <li>Continuous and discontinuous mode</li>
                  </ol>
                 </p>

              <h4> Materials and Equipment </h4>
                <p>Power supply, capacitor, inductor, transistor, diode, resistor</p>

              <h4>Procedure</h4>
                <div>
                  <ol>
                      <li>Make connections of power supply, capacitor, inductor, transistor and load resistor to build the buck converter.<br/>
                          Vin = 50 V, L = 400 μH, C = 100 μF, f = 20 kHz, R = 20 Ω</li>
                      <li>Check the circuit connections according to the schematics.</li>
                      <li>Varying duty cycle
                          <o1>
                            <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                            <li>Observe and measure the output voltage.</li>
                            <li>Vary the duty cycle from 20% to 80% with the interval of 10%.</li>
                            <li>Measure the output voltage for each of the duty cycle. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                          </o1>
                        </li>
                        <li>Varying switching frequency
                          <ol>
                            <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                            <li>Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                            <li>Increase the switching frequency to 50 kHz. Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                            <li>Decrease the switching frequency to 10 kHz. Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                          </ol>
                        </li>
                        <li>Varying load resistor
                          <ol>
                            <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                            <li>Measure the load current and output voltage when the load resistance is 20 Ω.</li>
                            <li>Measure the load current and output voltage when the load resistance is 40 Ω.</li>
                          </ol>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
